{"ts":"2026-09-10T00:54:27+03:00","event":"note","text":"Systemic defect of this round, third occurrence and now fixed at the source. Of the contamination incidents this round, three name the SAME cause in their own words: the reviewer opened a sibling artifact under data/reviews/ not for its content but to see the shape of the JSON - fw0_r4_q15 'to model the JSON shape before writing my own', fw1_r13_q10 'probing for a date-format example from a prior review', and probe_vision.6 on q17 in the same manner. The ban was in every brief and it was not the ban that failed: the roles had a real need and no legitimate way to meet it. Fixed in the prompts themselves rather than in my briefs, since the briefs are per-run and the prompts are not: a paragraph 'The contract is the example - do not go looking for one in that folder' now sits in all eight role prompts (three reviewers, five probes), saying that the neighbouring files are other reviewers' ANSWERS, that schema/review.py plus ROLE_ITEMS is the specification, and that a worked example should be generated from the contract rather than read from an answer"}
{"ts":"2026-09-10T00:54:37+03:00","event":"note","text":"fw1_r13_q10's declared violation weighed and the review KEPT - a judgement call, so here is the test I used rather than the conclusion alone. A find -exec cat printed full_window1.12.json, the previous review of this same window: it saw that file's envelope, budget, verdict FIX_INTERVALS, its five-line notes naming the fix [729.0, 734.6], and the opening of its first check; it states it did not read the remaining checks. Two things decide it. First, the leaked content does not contain this review's conclusion: 734.6 was already in front of it as the current evidence_span in its own sample.json, and what was genuinely new - r12's FIX_INTERVALS - is a verdict on a span that no longer exists. Second, and this is the test that matters, the leak points the OTHER WAY: it says the previous reviewer objected, so an objection here would be the suspicious direction and I would discard it; the review returned PASS against that pull, on a frame-by-frame fade measurement (734 full, 735 fading, 736 gone) that is nowhere in what it saw. Had it come back FIX_INTERVALS I would have thrown it out. Contrast fw0_r4_q15, discarded today: there the leak carried verdicts on all five items and the same frame timings, and the review reproduced them. q10 stays validated and line 607 is now closed by measurement rather than by my argument"}
