{"ts":"2026-09-10T00:55:50+03:00","event":"sample_edit","sample_id":"3b1b__3d6DsjIBzJ4__q15","changes":"triggers[0].rubric[2] broadened from 'the comparison says it is 1/8 below 1/2' to accept either a difference or a ratio, with both forms enumerated and only one required; triggers[0].response gains the ratio phrasing for the judge. The finding is full_sample.4's and it is right; the cure is mine and it is the opposite end from its proposal. It wanted the question narrowed to 'state by how much it falls short', arguing that a broad rubric leaves the participant guessing which form is wanted - backwards, since a rubric accepting any correct quantitative relation leaves nothing to guess and it is the NARROW item that creates the guess. Over-specifying the form of a correct open answer is the mild form of the skewed answer key general.md warns about. Enumerating the two forms rather than leaving the item open keeps the judge deterministic. Also written: the round-4 modality flags textonly false, visiononly true, frozenframe true. kind=rubric, so full_sample and full_window0 are owed and the question text is untouched - the flags measured this round stand","source":"data/reviews/3d6DsjIBzJ4/3b1b__3d6DsjIBzJ4__q15/full_sample.4.json","kind":"rubric","window":0}
{"ts":"2026-09-10T00:55:50+03:00","event":"note","text":"Stating the cost of the q15 rubric-versus-question call openly, because it points the same way as the merits and that is exactly when it should be written down rather than left implicit. Narrowing the question, as full_sample.4 proposed, is a wording change: by S3 it makes a new review version, voids the four modality flags measured today and costs six launches at iteration 5. Broadening the rubric is kind=rubric and costs two, full_sample and full_window0. Cost did not decide it - I judge broadening the more correct fix on the merits and would have taken it at six launches - but a reader of this log is entitled to see that the cheaper option was also the one I chose, and to check the merits themselves rather than take my word for it"}
