{"ts":"2026-09-10T01:13:09+03:00","event":"note","text":"fs_r5_q15's non-firing proposal on q15 rubric item 4 is DECLINED for this round and sent to the human list, and the reasoning is worth recording because a second independent reviewer has now raised it. The item reads 'do NOT count 1/2, which is the limit of the unmodified displayed series' and could, read in isolation, be taken as banning the value outright rather than as the new sum; the proposal is to name the object. Three things against applying it now. The reviewer itself says to apply it only if the rubric is being touched anyway and that it is not a condition of the PASS - and the rubric is not being touched anyway, this run IS the barrier. The exposure is judge-side rather than participant-side: the item's own gloss already says WHY 1/2 is excluded, and item 3 requires naming 1/2 as the limit, so a judge would have to ignore both to misapply it. And applying a two-word cosmetic change would owe a fresh full_sample plus full_window0 by the section-3 table, which is how a sample enters an endless polish loop. Round 3 reached the same conclusion independently. Recorded as a known, twice-raised ambiguity"}
{"ts":"2026-09-10T01:13:09+03:00","event":"note","text":"Screen index off by a second at the q15 trigger, confirmed independently twice and worth carrying to the report as a property of the tooling rather than of this sample. screen_text.md dates the first numerical series at 1076.0; real frames put it at 1077.0 - t=1074, 1075 and 1076 all still show the cos(x) scene with the 'Be careful' bubble, and 1077 shows the series already complete. fs_r5_q15 found it by sweep, fw0_r4b_q15 found the same hard cut between 1076 and 1077 by a dense pass, and sb_r4_q15's t_val rests on the same frames. This is exactly the failure mode the role prompts warn about when they allow the index for FINDING a moment and forbid citing it: a reviewer who had trusted the index would have proposed moving t_start a second early and would have been wrong"}
